            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "If there is no gpio select then how does the Flash access gets switched between BMC and Host ?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Tan Siewert",
                        "email": "tan@siewert.io",
                        "username": "sinuscosinustan"
                    },
                    "message": "The GXP is a custom SoC by HPE, and apparently they route the CPU to the BMC SoC directly. My suspicion is that they have some sort of SPI driver or mux internally which can't be controlled by the user.\n\nTheoretically, they also have something called `vrom`, which is a memory-mapped MTD that holds the BIOS and acts as a SPI for the host (i.e. the host boots from there.)\nHowever, we do not populate the `vrom` and it still works (still under discussion with HPE how this works within the SoC.)\n\nSo yes, this platform is cursed."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> If there is no gpio select then how does the Flash access gets switched between BMC and Host ?\n\nFrom my limited understanding it is not necessary to implement any extra hw logic for multiple masters to interact with a SPI flash, at least on an electrical level.\n\nAs long as the BMC and Host do not drive any shared signals at the same time, it should be fine?\n\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_Peripheral_Interface\n\nProbably the SPI controller and driver logic have to be known on both sides w.r.t their signal behavior to safely do it like that but for HPE maybe that what they did since they design the whole platform.\n\nUnfortunately i do not have any schematics of the hw so unclear how it is wired."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Tan Siewert",
                        "email": "tan@siewert.io",
                        "username": "sinuscosinustan"
                    },
                    "message": "Both tested and reviewed on HPE hardware and the ASRR ALTRAD8UD-1L2T."
                }
            ],
